+## Timing, which is not rhythm
+
+`analysis.summary()["timing"]` and the cover page report how far each note landed from the
+take's **own average pulse**: `onset_s` (where the DTW path put it) minus `score_onset_s`
+(the same note on a metronome at `implied_tempo_bpm`), in milliseconds. Per note it is
+`onset_error_ms` in the JSON; in summary it is the median, the 90th percentile, the signed
+mean (a take that drags reads positive), how many notes are outside 100 ms, and the worst one
+by name and bar.
+
+What it is good for: "this one ran away in bar 25" and "this take is uneven" — the drift is
+visible per note and per bar.
+
+What it is not:
+
+* **not a tempo check.** The reference line is the take's own average tempo, so a steady
+  performance at 92 BPM where the score says 100 reports near-zero timing spread and an
+  implied tempo of 92. The tempo IS the separate line above it.
+* **not a rhythm grade.** Onsets come from a DTW path over a *pitch* track, so a repeated
+  note has no boundary to find and a soft attack has nothing to detect. A duration is only
+  as good as that path. Real onset detection (`librosa.onset.onset_detect`, spectral flux) is
+  what a per-note rhythm mark would need.
+* **frame-level.** One `pyin` frame is 23 ms (512 samples at 22 050 Hz), so an onset is
+  resolved to about that. On the straight-tempo fixture the median spread measures **29 ms**;
+  on the real-score fixture, which has deliberate rubato, it measures **681 ms** — that is the
+  drift of a human take against its own average, not sloppiness.
+
+The cover page says this in as many words, so a report that lands in front of a student does
+not read as a rhythm grade.
+
 ## Known limitations
 
 * **Monophonic.** `pyin` tracks one fundamental, so a chord is measured as whichever
